<template>
  <main class="page shell">
    <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/journal">Journal</a> / Bean to bar</p>
    <article class="prose">
      <header>
        <p class="eyebrow">Process</p>
        <h1>From sack to bar in six days</h1>
        <p class="muted">
          <time datetime="2026-08-02">August 2, 2026</time> · 7 min read
        </p>
      </header>

      <p>
        A 60-kilo sack of cacao arrives on a Monday. By Saturday it is wrapped bars on
        a shelf. Here is where the time goes.
      </p>

      <h2>Day one: sorting</h2>
      <p>
        We spread the beans on a stainless table and pull out stones, flat beans, and
        the occasional twig. It takes two people about three hours for a sack. This is
        also the first quality check: smell the sack before you open it. If it smells
        like smoke or mold, stop.
      </p>

      <h2>Day two: roasting</h2>
      <p>
        Roast profiles are per origin. Esmeraldas likes a gentler curve with a longer
        development phase; Piura can take more heat without losing its fruit. We log
        bean temperature every thirty seconds and cup the result the next morning.
      </p>

      <h2>Day three: winnowing and grinding</h2>
      <p>
        The roasted beans go through a cracker and a winnower to separate nib from
        husk. From there the nibs go into the stone grinders with sugar and cocoa
        butter. The grinders run for 48 hours, which is the least interesting part of
        the process and the most important.
      </p>

      <h2>Day five: tempering and molding</h2>
      <p>
        After the conche we temper by hand for test batches, or run the machine for
        the house bar. Bars are molded, chilled for twenty minutes, then unmolded and
        rested overnight at room temperature.
      </p>

      <h2>Day six: wrapping</h2>
      <p>
        Each bar gets a batch stamp on the back of the wrapper. We keep a few bars from
        every batch in a cupboard and taste them at one, three, and six months. That
        is how we know whether a roast profile actually worked.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        “The wrapper is the last step, but the batch date is the first thing we write
        down.”
      </blockquote>
    </article>
  </main>
</template>
